# scripts/db-bootstrap.sh applies them to an empty database, then every migration numbered
# after the cut, in file-name order.
#
# Rules (src/lib/__tests__/db-baseline.test.ts holds them):
# - A new migration is always numbered after the cut. It applies on top of the baseline, and
#   on existing databases as before. Never add a file numbered at or below the cut.
# - Never edit schema.sql or seed.sql by hand. Regenerate them with
#   scripts/db-baseline-regenerate.mjs from DEV, read-only, and prove the result with
#   scripts/db-bootstrap.sh on an empty local stack. Do it when convenient: before a release,
#   or once a few dozen migrations sit past the cut. Migrations after the cut keep working
#   either way.
# - The baseline is the code's truth, not DEV's: what DEV holds that no migration makes and
#   no code uses is cut (EXCLUDE_* in scripts/db-baseline-exclude.mjs), and what DEV lacks
#   is applied on top in a scratch stack before the baseline is taken (DEV_GAPS, same file).
